# Native operator GUI i18n (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08): shipping **Win32 / Tauri / elevate /
launcher** operator GUIs must share one locale + catalog pattern so English
and German work now and more languages are a **data add**, not a code rewrite.
The admin SPA (`pim-offline-server/ui`) keeps **react-i18next** and is out of
scope for this rule.

## The rule

> **Native operator GUIs resolve locale as: stored `UI_LOCALE` AppConfig
> preference → OS UI language → English. Strings for shared chrome live in
> `pim-app-config-i18n` locale JSON (`bundles/gui/<tag>/chrome.json`), not
> `#ifdef` / env vars. Source strings in code and default catalogs stay US
> English. Translated visible labels keep matching accessible names
> (Section 508).**

## Required pattern

1. Depend on `pim-app-config-i18n`.
2. Call `resolve_locale(preference)` with the trimmed `UI_LOCALE` value (or
   `None` when unset).
3. Use `catalog::gui::chrome_for(loc)` (or product-specific catalogs under
   `bundles/`) for operator-visible chrome.
4. When applying a translated label, set `aria-label` / Win32 accessible name
   to the same string.

## Forbidden

- `PIM_LOCALE` / `$env:…` / `LANG` as AIC product configuration for GUI locale
  (OS APIs for UI language are fine; inventing AIC env vars is not —
  [`no-environment-variables.mdc`](no-environment-variables.mdc)).
- Defaulting the UI to German when preference and OS are English/unset.
- Scattering German (or other) string literals behind `#[cfg]` in product GUIs.
- Claiming i18n “done” for the admin SPA via this crate.

## Canonical crate

`pim-app-config/crates/pim-app-config-i18n` — see
`pim-offline-server-configurator-tauri/docs/operator-gui-i18n.md` for the
first Tauri proof consumer.

